# px66, kasumi: the clean-flag kill row, by notes' killboot recipe (the session kill.sh on a disk of tools/mkdisk --gpt; QEMU's qemu-run killed by its pid the moment the serial says 'part: waiting'; dumpe2fs -h of partition 2 on the host), 2026-10-23
## k1: kernel b2 (px63's ext2: the mounted state left in the write-back cache), 15:50
made: Filesystem state: clean;Mount count: 0;
qemu-run (pid 734309) killed, status 143
after the kill: Filesystem state: clean;Mount count: 0;      <- RED: the volume was mounted
second boot (the boot session on the same disk): fstab: /dev/vda2 on /home: ext2, read-write, write-back
  ext2: vda2: …; clean, mount 1; read-write
after the second boot's exit: Filesystem state: clean;Mount count: 1;
## k1's serial, the kill boot's last lines
fstab: /dev/vda2 on /home: ext2, read-write, write-back
ext2: vda2: 15867 blocks of 4096 bytes, 1 groups, 15872 inodes of 256 bytes, features compat 0038 incompat 0002 ro_compat 0003; 14861 blocks and 15860 inodes free; clean, mount 1; read-write
run 1: /bin/sh /etc/part/kill.sh
part: start
part: waiting
## k2: kernel b3 (074cd4d8: the mounted state written and flushed at the mount; the clean state after the flush), 15:51
made: Filesystem state: clean;Mount count: 0;
qemu-run (pid 738253) killed, status 143
after the kill: Filesystem state: not clean;Mount count: 1;
second boot: ext2: vda2: …; not cleanly unmounted, mount 2; read-write
after the second boot's exit: Filesystem state: not clean;Mount count: 2;
